\title{Upper-Bound-Driven Core and Truss Reductions\\for the Maximum Clique Problem}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 16, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a general mathematical framework for strengthening reduction rules for the Maximum Clique Problem by means of clique upper-bound functions. For a simple graph $G=(V,E)$ and a region $S\subseteq V$, an upper-bound function $U(S)$ dominates the cardinality of every finite clique contained in $S$. Given a proposed pattern $D$, every clique containing $D$ consists of $D$ together with a clique in its common neighborhood. This yields
\[
 |C|\le |D|+U(S\cap N(D)).
\]
We prove the associated exclusion criterion: if the right-hand side is below a target $k$, no clique of size at least $k$ in $S$ contains $D$. Singleton and two-vertex instances give upper-bound-enhanced core and truss tests. We define certified vertex peeling and prove that one step, and hence every finite sequence of steps, preserves every finite clique of size at least $k$. We also show that the pointwise minimum of valid upper bounds remains valid. Algorithms, complexity parameters, applications, and numerical examples are discussed. The framework separates correctness from the particular bound used and clarifies the relation between classical local-size reductions and stronger structural tests.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
A clique in a simple undirected graph is a set of vertices whose distinct members are pairwise adjacent. Finding the largest clique is a difficult global optimization problem even though checking a proposed clique is elementary. Exact methods consequently rely on reductions that remove vertices or patterns incapable of participating in a sufficiently large solution.

If an incumbent clique has size $k-1$, only cliques of size at least $k$ can improve it. Classical core reduction observes that a vertex in a $k$-clique has at least $k-1$ neighbors. A truss-style argument observes that the endpoints of an edge in a $k$-clique have at least $k-2$ common neighbors. These counts ignore adjacency among those neighbors. High degree need not imply a large clique in the neighborhood, and many common neighbors may be internally fragmented.

We replace raw neighborhood size by any valid upper bound on clique size in the relevant neighborhood. The bound may arise from coloring, degeneracy, a relaxation, an exact local computation, or a combination of methods. Correctness depends only on its upper-bound contract, allowing algorithmic strength and computational cost to be varied independently.

Our contributions are a theorem for combining bounds pointwise, a general pattern-extension inequality, singleton and pair specializations, and preservation of all target-size cliques under finite certified vertex peeling. The edge result supplies a local truss certificate; a full evolving edge-peeling process and implementation-specific running times remain extensions.

\section{Definitions}
\begin{definition}[Simple graph and clique]
A simple undirected graph is $G=(V,E)$ with symmetric, irreflexive adjacency, written $x\sim y$. A set $C\subseteq V$ is a clique if $x\sim y$ for every pair of distinct vertices $x,y\in C$. Whenever $|C|$ appears, $C$ is finite.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Common neighborhood]
For $D\subseteq V$, its common neighborhood is
\[
 N(D)=\{x\in V: \text{for every }w\in D,\ x\sim w\}.
\]
For a singleton this is the ordinary open neighborhood; for a pair it is the set of common neighbors.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Clique upper-bound function]
A function $U:2^V\to\NN$ is a clique upper-bound function if every finite clique $C\subseteq S$ satisfies
\[
 |C|\le U(S).
\]
No monotonicity assumption is required.
\end{definition}

Examples include the cardinality bound $U(S)=|S|$ on finite sets, the number of colors in any proper coloring of the induced graph on $S$, the degeneracy plus one, and the exact clique number when it is affordable to compute. A coloring is valid because a clique uses at most one vertex of each color.

\section{Combining upper bounds}
\begin{theorem}[Minimum Combination Theorem]
If $U_1$ and $U_2$ are clique upper-bound functions, then
\[
 U(S)=\min\{U_1(S),U_2(S)\}
\]
is a clique upper-bound function.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For every finite clique $C\subseteq S$, validity gives $|C|\le U_1(S)$ and $|C|\le U_2(S)$. Hence $|C|$ is at most their minimum.
\end{proof}

The same argument combines any finite nonempty family of bounds. Thus independently developed estimates can cooperate without a new soundness argument. In an implementation, a cheap estimate may be evaluated first and a stronger expensive estimate deferred until needed.

\section{The pattern-extension principle}
\begin{theorem}[Clique Extension Bound]
Let $U$ be a clique upper-bound function. Suppose $C$ is a finite clique, $D\subseteq C$, and $C\subseteq S$. Then
\[
 |C|\le |D|+U(S\cap N(D)).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Set $R=C\setminus D$. As a subset of $C$, the set $R$ is a finite clique. Every $x\in R$ belongs to $S$. Moreover, for each $w\in D$, the vertices $x$ and $w$ are distinct members of $C$, so $x\sim w$. Therefore $R\subseteq S\cap N(D)$, and validity of $U$ gives
\[
 |R|\le U(S\cap N(D)).
\]
Since $D$ and $R$ are disjoint and have union $C$, we have $|C|=|D|+|R|$, proving the inequality.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Failed Extension Test]
Let $k\in\NN$. If
\[
 |D|+U(S\cap N(D))<k,
\]
then no finite clique $C\subseteq S$ with $|C|\ge k$ contains $D$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
If such a clique existed, the extension bound would give
\[
 k\le |C|\le |D|+U(S\cap N(D))<k,
\]
a contradiction.
\end{proof}

Failure is decisive, whereas passing the test does not assert that an extension exists: an upper bound may exceed the true clique number. The pattern need not separately be assumed to be a clique. If it is contained in a clique, that property follows; if it is not a clique, no clique contains it.

\section{Core and truss specializations}
\begin{theorem}[Vertex Core Test]
Let $C\subseteq S$ be a finite clique, let $v\in C$, and suppose $|C|\ge k$. Then
\[
 k\le 1+U(S\cap N(\{v\})).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply the Clique Extension Bound to $D=\{v\}$ and combine $k\le |C|$ with $|D|=1$.
\end{proof}

Thus a vertex is safely deleted when
\[
 1+U(S\cap N(\{v\}))<k.
\]
With $U(X)=|X|$, this is the classical degree test. Structural estimates can be strictly stronger: a vertex may have many neighbors while its induced neighborhood has small clique number.

\begin{theorem}[Edge Truss Test]
Let $C\subseteq S$ be a finite clique. If distinct $u,v\in C$ and $|C|\ge k$, then
\[
 k\le 2+U(S\cap N(\{u,v\})).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply the Clique Extension Bound to $D=\{u,v\}$, whose cardinality is two.
\end{proof}

Consequently, if the displayed right-hand side is less than $k$, no target-size clique can contain both endpoints. With the cardinality bound this recovers the condition that an edge in a $k$-clique has at least $k-2$ common neighbors. A sharper bound measures whether those common neighbors can themselves form a large clique.

For any finite pattern $D$, define its extension score by
\[
 B_S(D)=|D|+U(S\cap N(D)).
\]
The Failed Extension Test excludes $D$ whenever $B_S(D)<k$. Larger patterns can yield tighter completion regions but require more tests and more expensive intersections.

\section{Certified core peeling}
\begin{definition}[Certified core-peeling step]
Fix $k$ and $U$. A set $T$ is obtained from $S$ by one certified core-peeling step if some $v\in S$ satisfies
\[
 1+U(S\cap N(\{v\}))<k
\]
and $T=S\setminus\{v\}$. The certificate is evaluated in the current region $S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[One-Step Clique Preservation]
If $T$ is obtained from $S$ by a certified core-peeling step and $C\subseteq S$ is a finite clique with $|C|\ge k$, then $C\subseteq T$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $v$ be deleted. If $v\in C$, the Vertex Core Test gives
\[
 k\le 1+U(S\cap N(\{v\})),
\]
contradicting the strict deletion certificate. Hence $v\notin C$, so $C\subseteq S\setminus\{v\}=T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite Core-Peeling Preservation]
Let $S_0,S_1,\ldots,S_t$ be a finite sequence in which each $S_{i+1}$ is obtained from $S_i$ by a certified core-peeling step. If $C\subseteq S_0$ is a finite clique with $|C|\ge k$, then $C\subseteq S_i$ for every $i$, and in particular $C\subseteq S_t$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $i$. The base case is the assumption $C\subseteq S_0$. The induction step is exactly the One-Step Clique Preservation Theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Decision equivalence]
The initial region $S_0$ contains a clique of size at least $k$ if and only if the residual region $S_t$ does.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Every clique in $S_t$ is in $S_0$ because the sets only shrink. Conversely, every qualifying clique in $S_0$ survives by finite peeling preservation.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Optimum preservation above the target]
If the maximum clique size in $S_0$ is at least $k$, then $S_0$ and $S_t$ have equal maximum clique size.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
A maximum clique in $S_0$ survives, while passing to a subset cannot create a larger clique.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms and complexity}
For a finite graph, the direct core-peeling algorithm maintains an active set $S$. It finds a vertex satisfying the deletion criterion, removes it, and repeats until no such vertex remains. At most $n=|V|$ deletions occur. If every stage naively scans all active vertices and one bound evaluation costs at most $T_U(n,m)$, a coarse running-time estimate is
\[
 O(n^2T_U(n,m)+n^2),
\]
apart from graph-representation costs. Queues, dependency tracking, bitsets, incremental colorings, and batch updates can improve this substantially. The preservation proof applies to any implementation in which each deletion has the required current-set certificate.

For pattern exclusion, select a family $\mathcal D$ and evaluate $B_S(D)$ for each $D\in\mathcal D$. Singleton failures cause vertex deletion; pair failures cause edge exclusion or search restrictions. Cost scales with the number of patterns, common-neighborhood construction, and bound evaluation.

An adaptive cascade can combine a cheap bound $U_1$ and a costly bound $U_2$. Compute $b_1=U_1(X)$ first. If $|D|+b_1<k$, reject immediately. Otherwise compute $b_2=U_2(X)$ and use $\min\{b_1,b_2\}$. The Minimum Combination Theorem guarantees correctness regardless of evaluation order.

\section{Numerical examples}
\begin{example}[Coloring strengthens degree reduction]
Let $k=5$, and suppose $v$ has eight active neighbors. Cardinality yields $1+8=9$, so degree reduction cannot remove $v$. If the induced neighborhood has a proper three-coloring, then $U(S\cap N(v))\le3$, and
\[
 1+U(S\cap N(v))\le4<5.
\]
Thus $v$ is safely deleted.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Structure strengthens edge support]
Let $k=5$, and suppose adjacent $u,v$ have six common active neighbors. Raw support passes because $2+6=8$. If the common-neighbor graph is bipartite, its clique number is at most two, so
\[
 2+U(S\cap N(\{u,v\}))\le4<5.
\]
The edge belongs to no $5$-clique.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Iterative effects]
A first deletion may shrink another vertex's active neighborhood enough to make its score fall below $k$. Such cascades remain safe: every member of a clique of size at least $k$ passes the test at every stage, and the preservation theorem ensures that the entire clique remains active.
\end{example}

\section{Applications}
In branch-and-bound, an incumbent of size $L$ sets $k=L+1$. Certified peeling reduces each residual subproblem before branching, without removing any possible improvement. If fewer than $k$ vertices remain, the branch is impossible.

For the decision problem asking whether a $k$-clique exists, the original and peeled regions are equivalent. For enumeration of all cliques of size at least $k$, every qualifying clique retains all its vertices. Similar reasoning applies to compatibility systems in which vertices denote choices and edges denote pairwise coexistence.

Network applications include collaboration, communication, biological, and transaction graphs. Raw degree can confuse a large diffuse neighborhood with a cohesive one. A coloring or degeneracy bound recognizes internal fragmentation and can eliminate candidates that count-based rules retain.

\section{Discussion}
The framework is modular: correctness relies on the defining contract for $U$, while performance depends on how that contract is implemented. Degree and common-neighbor support are coarse special cases that replace the clique number of the completion region by its cardinality. Better estimates strengthen reduction without changing its logic.

Strict inequality is essential. Equality leaves open a clique of size exactly $k$. Certificates must also refer to the relevant current region. Bounds on a larger region may be safe but weaker; unjustifiably restricting the completion region can be unsound.

For finite graphs, deletion of one vertex per step terminates after at most $|V|$ steps. Precise implementation-level complexity requires a fixed representation, bound algorithm, and update policy. The Edge Truss Test proves the local certificate for edge exclusion, but complete iterative edge peeling requires an explicit evolving edge relation and its own preservation induction.

\section{Future work}
Natural extensions include iterative edge-deletion peeling for upper-bound-enhanced trusses; radius-$d$ edge neighborhoods that trade greater reduction strength for additional cost; concrete greedy-coloring and degeneracy bounds with explicit complexity analyses; combined core/truss fixed points; and semantics-preserving graph transformations such as repeated structions. Another question is how to schedule several bounds with different costs and expected strengths. Their minimum is always sound, so scheduling can optimize expected time without changing correctness.

\section{Conclusion}
A clique containing $D$ can be completed only inside $N(D)$. Bounding that completion region yields
\[
 |C|\le |D|+U(S\cap N(D)),
\]
which in turn gives a failed-extension test, enhanced core and truss criteria, and preservation of every target-size clique under repeated certified vertex peeling. Pointwise minima make independent upper bounds composable. These results provide a compact foundation for replacing local counts by structural estimates while safely reducing the search space of maximum-clique algorithms.

